/*
  Welcome to the GD++ language!
  GD++ is a Domain Specific Language (DSL) that targets regular C++.
  It is designed with one purpose in mind: enabling easy, boilerplate-free C++ development for the Godot engine.
  This file is a demonstration of the basic syntax and capabilities of GD++.
  For example, this text is part of a multi-line comment. Multi-line comments start with /* and end with a matching */.
  Emphasis on the *matching* part. So this line is still part of the comment. This is more similar to Rust than C++.
*/

// And this is a normal single-line comment.
// We will be using a lot of these in this tutorial.

// Part I. Lexical scopes.

// GD++ features 4 distinct lexical scopes (not counting auxiliary scopes like comments, quotations, etc.):
// 1. The class declaration scope.
//    The syntax here resembles GDScript very closely (though it is *not* GDScript, just like GDScript itself relembles Python but is not Python).
// 2. The property declaration scope.
//    This is a very niche scope that only exists to group together property getters, setters and declarations.
// 3. The enum declaration scope.
//    Simply a list of enumeration names, with optional integer values.
//    They don't have to be comma-separated, simply separating with a space or newline is already enough, though comma separated also works.
// 4. The C++ implementation scope.
//    In this scope, arbitrary C++ code is allowed.
//    There are a few minor modifications, most notably comments are still parsed by GD++ (and stripped out of the generated C++).
//    This means multiline comments work like they do in GD++.

// Part II. Classes.

// There may or may not be a single class-level definition that goes at the very top of the file like so:

class_name MyNode
extends Node3D  // If not specified, inherits from RefCounted.
// Here we are in the class declaration scope.

// Additionally, the GD++ file can contain inline classes defined like so:
class MyNode1 {
  extends Node
  // Here we are in the class declaration scope.
}

class MyRefCounted1 {
  // By default, inherits from RefCounted, like in GDScript.
}

// There is no difference between file-level and inline classes, it's purely preference.
// The GD++ compiler puts all these classes on the same level in the generated file.
// There are no nested classes in GD++.

// If there is no file-level class, GD++ enters a limited class declaration scope.
// Only inline classes (and other inline entities like enums and externs, see below) are allowed.
// However, since we've declared the file-level class, we're in full class declaration scope here.

// Part III. Embedding C++.

// GD++ is designed to emit C++, and so it makes it possible to embed arbitrary C++ code anywhere in the file.
// This feature is extremely powerful, but should be used sparingly, since it also invites verbose boilerplate.
// We use two keywords: decl and impl. They can also be combined into a decl impl.

decl {
  // This block of C++ code will be embedded into the class definition in the header file.
  // To embed a block outside of the class definition (and the godot::namespace),
  // use decl before class_name (decl, impl and decl impl are the only allowed constructs that can go before class_name).
  void private_function();
}

impl {
  // This block of C++ code will be embedded into the source file, inside the godot:: namespace.
  // To embed outside of the godot:: namespace, use impl before class_name.
  void This::private_function() {
    // Note the use of `This`: GD++ will emit a special #define (and an #undef at the end) that resolves it to the name of the current class.
    // This is similar to how Self works in Rust.
    if (Engine::is_editor_hint()) return;

    // gd is another shorthand, this is a classdef that points to UtilityFunctions,
    // so the "global scope" functions in Godot terminology.
    gd::print("Hello, world!");
  }
}

// You can combine decl and impl to embed a single block into both header and source, like so:

decl impl {
  #define FOO 42
  // Don't forget to #undef at the end of the file!
}

// Note: if you want to embed into a public / protected section of the class, simply define another section inside of the decl block, i.e.

decl {
 public:
  void public_function();  // Visible from all C++ classes, but not exposed to Godot.
}

// Part IV. Godot types.

// In GD++, you will work with Godot's type system.
// To each Godot type there's a corresponding canonical C++ type:
// bool -> bool
// int -> int64_t
// float -> float64_t (GD++-provided typedef for double).
// Non-object Godot types, e.g. Vector3 -> C++ structs with identical names, provided by the Godot C++ Bindings.
// Non-refcounted types, e.g. Node -> pointers, e.g. Node*
// Refcounted types, e.g. Resource -> references, e.g. Ref<Resource>
// GD++ enums, e.g. MyEnum -> generated enum class C++ enums, so MyEnum::VALUE_1 are available etc, see more about enums below.
// Non-refcounted externs -> ExtPtr<MyExtern>, see more about externs below.
// Refcounted externs -> ExtRef<MyExtern>, see more about externs below.

// Part V. Constants and enums.

// GDExtensions only allows integer constants to be exposed to Godot, so we only support them.
// The definition syntax is

enum THE_ANSWER = 42

// Note that there is no const keyword in GD++.

// Enums in GD++ are slightly different from C++ / Godot / GDScript.
// Enums are global-scoped types in GD++, so e.g.

enum Suit {
  DIAMONDS
  CLUBS
  SPADES
  HEARTS
}

enum Rank {
  JACK = 0
  QUEEN
  KING
}

// Is an inline enum definition that is visible anywhere from the GD++ package.
// When GD++ registers enums with Godot, they must belong to a class, because Godot enums belong to a class.
// GD++ will generate an identical enum definition for all classes that make use of it in the GD++ APIs, that is,
// in any variables, function arguments, function return values or signal arguments.
// Additionally, if the file-level class defines an inline enum, that gets exported too.

// The exported values are called SUIT_DIAMONDS, SUIT_CLUBS, etc. (PascalCase is converted in to UPPER_SNAKE_CASE automatically).

// You can force a class to export an enum or to not export an enum via
import Suit  // This is redundant, since Suit is already defined in this class, it's as a demonstration only.
noimport Rank  // Rank will not be exported as an enum of this class (but may be exported as an enum of other classes that use / import it).

// Finally, similarly to class_name, there's enum_name that makes the whole file a enum and enters the enum scope. It's almost never used, but kept around for consistency.
// E.g. the whole file could be just
// > enum_name Foo
// > FOO1
// > FOO2
// Such a file would export no classes to Godot, but other GD++ files can use the enum.

// Part VI. Functions.

// Functions are defined with GDScript-inspired syntax:

func greet_user(name: String) -> void {
  // C++ implementation.
  gd::print("Hello ", name, "!");
}

// Arguments and return types implicitly assume Variant if nothing is provided.

// GD++ automatically exposes them as public functions in C++, and registers them as Godot functions in the generated _bind_methods.
// All types provided are Godot types. They become canonically mapped C++ classes in C++ code.

// We can define script-virtual functions with the @virtual attribute:

@virtual
func _foo() -> int {
  return 0;
}

@virtual
func _bar() -> int  // This is also OK, GD++ will generate a default implementation that returns the default value of the type, which is 0.

func foo_plus_bar() -> int {
  return _foo() + _bar();
}

// Later, we can overwrite them in GDScript, e.g.
// func _foo() -> int:
//   return 42

// To override already provided engine virtual functions, use @override instead:
@override
func _ready() -> void {
  // Caveat: the function signature must match *exactly*, e.g. omitting -> void will result in a compilation error, since _ready does not return Variant.
  gd::print("My node is ready.");
}

// Functions can be marked as const with the @const attribute:

decl {
  int64_t my_value_;
}

@const
func get_my_value() -> int {
  return my_value;
}

// Finally, to have a static function, mark it with the @static attribute:

@static
func my_static_func() -> void {
  gd::print("Hello!");
}

// Attributes can be combined when it makes sense, e.g. @virtual @const is allowed.

// Part VII. Constructors and destructors.

// In GD++, each class can have at most one constructor and at most one destructor.
// They must not accept any arguments. Syntax:

ctor {
  gd::print("This message will show up every time something creates MyNode.");
}

dtor {
  gd::print("This message will show up every time a MyNode is deleted.");
}

// Part VIII. Signals.

// Define signals with GDScript-inspired syntax:

signal something_happened(a: int)
signal something_else_happened

// Now you can emit them from C++ code, e.g.

func emit_both_signals() -> void {
  emit something_happened(42);
  emit something_else_happened();
}

// Generated signal functions emit signals and return a [[nodiscard]] struct.
// `emit` is simply a convenient way to tell the compiler to use the value without using it.
// This is an intentional design choice: we want emitting signals to be visually different from calling functions in code.

// Part IX. Variables.

// There are two ways to define variables: a simple variable and a property.
// Under the hood, they are exactly the same thing, it's just that the syntax is different.

var simple_var: int  // Default-initializes to 0.
var another_simple_var: int = 5  // Default-initializes to 5.

// Variables can be default-assigned a C++ expression, or a block of C++ code that runs on initialization, e.g.

@onready
var child_node_1: Node3D = get_node<Node3D>("MyNode1")

@onready
var child_node_2: Node3D = {
  Node3D* res = memnew(Node3D);
  add_child(res);
  return res;
}

// The @onready here isn't required - it only makes the initialization run in _ready as opposed to in the constructor.

// Simple variables are visible from C++ under the canonically mapped types with the same name, e.g. simple_var is a field of MyNode of type int64_t.
// They are exposed to Godot via generated getters and setters.

// Properties are variables with explicitly defined getters and setters. Either a getter or a setter may be omitted, making the variable get-only or set-only.

// This property can never be set to a non-negative number from Godot.
var my_property: int {
  decl {
    // Goes in the private section of the class.
    int64_t my_property = 0;
  }
  get {
    // Generated public method is: int64_t get_my_property() const.
    return my_property;
  }
  set(val) {
    // Generated public method is: void set_my_property(int64_t my_property);
    if (val < 0) val = 0;
    my_property = val;
  }
}

// Part X. Includes.

// GD++ simplifies writing #include by not requiring the programmer to write any of them.
// During compilation, it will scan the available C++ files and build a table of includes for both Godot/GDExtension-provided and user-defined classes.
// It will auto-include all types that have been explicitly spelled out anywhere in the code, this includes public API types, or just types spelled out in C++ code.

// In other words, to include a class, simply use it.

// There are rare exceptions where this might not work, e.g. when introspecting the contents of an object without naming its type in code.
// To force-include, use

import MeshInstance3D  // This will force-#include the appropriate header.

// To disable including a class (almost always leads to compilation errors, but the user is still empowered to do so):

noimport MeshInstance2D

// Finally, there's always the top-level decl { #include <...> }. Try to avoid that at all costs though.

// Part XI. Annotations.

// Annotations are structured data attached to the AST.
// By convention they go before the declaration they are attached to.
// The syntax is @annotation(param1, ...), or just @annotation also works if there are no params.
// E.g.

@export_range(0, 100, 1)
var my_range_var: int

// There's a special kind of attribute with a different syntax, which is the documentation comment.
// Similarly to normal comments, there are single-line and multi-line documentation comments:

/// This is a variable.
var some_var: int

/**
  This function has a
  long
  description.
*/
func foo() -> void {}

// Part XII. Externs.

// GD++ packages can not depend on other packages at compile time, but sometimes a runtime dependency is needed.
// GD++ makes it easy for programmers to declare runtime dependencies with externs.
// Here we will do so with inline externs, but there's also a file-level extern_name similar to class_name and enum_name.

extern Terrain {
  extends Node
  // Note the absence of definitions - externs can *NOT* have function bodies, property bodies, or variable default values.
  @const func foo() -> void
  @deferred func bar() -> void
  var baz: int
  signal done(a: bool, b: int)
}

// In C++ and in the public Godot API, externs are just objects of their base class.
// So ExtPtr<Terrain> will actually just contain a Node* under the hood.

// We can expose externs e.g. like so:
@export var my_extern: Terrain

// In Godot, my_extern will be of type Node. However, GD++ will generate the property bindings that mention the "Terrain" type.
// So e.g. in the Godot editor, the inspector will only allow assigning nodes of type "Terrain" to it.

// GD++ generates wrappers for the externs, making it possible to use them as regular types, however, whenever a method is called on an extern,
// Godot uses string-based resolution, i.e. calling foo() on an ExtPtr<Terrain> is equivalent to calling call("foo") on the underlying Node*.

// The @deferred attribute (which can only be used in an extern) makes the calls go through call_deferred instead of a plain call.

// With externs, packages can have runtime dependencies on other packages and even on other non-GD++ GDExtensions.
// However, externs are by design slow. Minimize their usage as much as possible.
